; `for` has four shapes and one body expression, and a form that
; is none of them is a PARSE error rather than a type error.
;
; `(for i lo hi body)`, `(for i lo hi step body)` and `(for x xs body)`
; are told apart by ARITY, because a parser knows no types - four
; operands after the binder is the stepped range, three the range,
; two the container. That forces the one-body rule: a variadic body
; would make `(for x xs a b)` ambiguous with the range, and `{ ... }`
; is how several expressions are sequenced.
;
; So a sixth element cannot belong to any shape, and the expectation
; string names all four rather than only the one the parser happened
; to be attempting. `.axbad` because a parse error stops the file, so
; this case carries exactly one malformed form.
(import IO)

(:: main Int)

(fn (main) (for i 0 5 1 2 3))
